{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And blessed be El Elyon.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Nedarim 32b) that he was punished because he delayed the blessing of HaShem and preceded the blessing of Avraham, and the priesthood was taken from him. And it is possible that Shem's reason is that he followed the order of the matter, for Avraham on his own recognized his Creator and strove in his faith, may He be blessed, and the blessing befits him; and afterward he blessed HaShem, who was appeased through him — and so it is fitting to do. And he came to teach that the Holy One, blessed be He, is appeased only through one who first appeases himself to Him — and this is what is meant by ״בָּרוּךְ אַבְרָם לְאֵל עֶלְיוֹן״, for he prepared himself to be a servant to El Elyon, and therefore ״בָּרוּךְ הוּא אֵל עֶלְיוֹן אֲשֶׁר מִגֵּן״ etc. And this approach is justified only when the blessed one is mentioned first. And accordingly Shem was not punished, but rather he himself gave [the blessing] to Avraham, as I explained in connection with the verse ״וְהוּא כֹהֵן״. Or it is possible that he was punished on account of what is heard from the surface level of the statement, which makes it appear that he was not careful to place the servant before his Master — even though he intended it for the good. And we find that HaShem punishes for things like this in several places."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:15.282223488Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}